# SECTION 7 — APPENDICES

## 7.1 Appendix I — ADR-001 Architecture Decision Record

### ADR-001 — Ratification of the React 19 SPA Client and MySQL/XAMPP Data Tier

| Attribute | Value |
|---|---|
| **Status** | Accepted |
| **Date** | 23 September 2026 |
| **Decision Owner** | Zapico, Zaki — Project Manager / Lead Developer |
| **Supersedes** | Flutter/SQLite client architecture; hosted cloud data tier |
| **Related Documents** | Project Plan Rev 2.3 §2.1, §2.2, §3.2; System Architecture & Technical Specifications §3.4; Letter of Agreement §1.1 |

#### Context

At the time of the comprehensive document and code audit, three client implementations existed simultaneously in the working environment: two independent Flutter applications using local SQLite persistence, and one React 19 single-page application. The project baseline specified a React 19 SPA served from the host laptop's XAMPP stack against MySQL 8.x, with IndexedDB used only as a client-side transaction queue.

Both Flutter implementations failed their own acceptance criteria. Neither could transmit a payload that the PHP backend would accept, because both posted a flat order map while the backend required a nested `{"order": {…}}` envelope, and neither ever transmitted the `items` array the backend required. The larger of the two additionally failed to launch, because it did not register its state-management provider at the application root while its screens requested that provider. It further wrote line items against a literal order identifier of zero without awaiting the database insert, orphaning every line item, and contained a 191-line checkout implementation that was never imported.

The React application carried an undocumented dependency on Supabase — hosted PostgreSQL with GoTrue authentication, accessed over the public internet. The word "Supabase" appears in none of the fourteen project documents. That application contained no PHP, no MySQL, no Apache, and no local-area-network code.

#### Decision

The React 19 / TanStack Router / Vite 8 / Tailwind CSS 4 client, served locally by Apache on the host laptop and persisting to MySQL 8.x through PHP 8.x PDO APIs, is hereby **ratified as the sole implementation path** for VenueVue.

Both Flutter codebases are frozen and moved to the `archive/flutter-prototype` branch. They are retained solely as interface design references and receive no further development, feature work, or defect remediation.

The Supabase dependency in the React implementation is removed during Sprint 4 and replaced by the local PHP API specified in Project Plan §4.

#### Rationale

1. **Single-source updating.** Deploying a rebuilt `dist/` folder onto the host laptop updates the entire application across all barista and administrative tablets simultaneously upon browser refresh. Flutter requires manual side-loading on every tablet, and emergency code patches require physical USB access to each device — an impractical operational burden during fast-paced event setups.

2. **Universal tablet compatibility.** Chrome and Safari both reach the React SPA. Flutter introduces build and OS-version divergence across the client's mixed Android and iPadOS device pool, requiring per-platform artefacts and per-platform testing.

3. **Single authoritative store.** MySQL 8.x on the host laptop is the sole system of record. A per-tablet SQLite database would create N divergent data stores requiring manual reconciliation — which is precisely the manual labour VenueVue exists to eliminate — and would place inventory truth on devices that can be lost, stolen, or left unsynchronised.

4. **Contractual alignment.** Letter of Agreement §1.1 specifies "a standalone local server environment (PHP/MySQL) running on a primary laptop workstation that creates a local Wi-Fi hotspot for mobile tablet endpoints." The ratified stack satisfies that specification directly; a Flutter client with per-device SQLite does not, because it relocates authoritative data onto the tablets.

5. **Effort feasibility.** The approved 650-hour budget across three developers assumes a single implementation. With three implementations in flight, the budget is not attainable and no artefact reaches completion. This is the primary measurable root cause of the project's assessed 12–15% completion against a 38% elapsed timeline.

#### Correction to the Historical Record

Revision 2.0 of the Project Plan asserted that "native mobile builds (such as Flutter APKs) were evaluated during early planning." This claim is not supported by the earliest artefact: the Software Engineering I plan (Rev 3.0, 9 May 2026) contains no reference to Flutter and selected a browser-delivered HTML5/CSS3/JavaScript client.

The accurate historical sequence, which replaces the earlier narrative in all VenueVue documentation, is as follows:

> The project's initial architecture was a browser-delivered HTML5/CSS3/JavaScript client. During Revision 2.0 planning, the team evaluated native Flutter APK deployment and rejected it for the operational reasons recorded above — manual side-loading, cross-platform build divergence, and physical USB access required for emergency patches. A React 19 Single Page Application compiled with Vite 8 and styled with Tailwind CSS 4 was selected in its place.

This correction is material because the earlier narrative invited a legitimate challenge from an academic evaluator: a rejected-technology rationale cannot rest on an evaluation that the project's own earliest document does not record.

#### Consequences

**Positive.** Single-source deployment across all tablets; elimination of per-platform build management; a single authoritative data store with no reconciliation requirement; alignment with the signed Letter of Agreement; and an attainable effort budget.

**Negative.** The system acquires a documented single point of failure in the host laptop, which is disclosed contractually by the amendment to Letter of Agreement §1.4 and managed operationally by the failure-state definitions in Project Plan §2.2.2 and the recovery procedures in §2.2.3.

**Retired capabilities.** The `sqflite` dependency and both `database_helper.dart` implementations are retired. Client-side durability becomes the responsibility of the IndexedDB queue specified in Project Plan §2.3.4.

**Terminology consequence.** The term "offline" throughout all VenueVue documentation denotes the **absence of WAN/Internet connectivity**, not autonomous operation of individual tablets. All documentation, source-code commentary, and oral defence must use this definition.

#### Binding Cloud Exclusion

**No hosted database, managed authentication provider, content delivery network, or third-party cloud service shall be introduced into any build of VenueVue.**

The Rationale clause of Project Plan §1.2.1 establishes that cloud-dependent POS systems are unusable at TravelBean MNL's venues. Any dependency on a hosted backend falsifies the project's central premise, breaches Letter of Agreement §1.1, introduces undisclosed third-party data processing under R.A. 10173, and cannot be demonstrated at an off-grid event venue.

Compliance is verified at every phase gate by executing a complete sales cycle while monitoring outbound network traffic. The gate passes only on observing **zero outbound WAN calls** during the cycle. Compliance is additionally evidenced by the absence of any cloud SDK in the build manifest and by an explicit deployment-environment review at gate G10.

#### Protected Scope Consequence

The schema frozen in Revision 2.2 could not deliver the client's headline requirement. Revision 2.3 expands the core schema to **nine tables** by adding `EVENTS`, `ORDERS.event_id`, `ORDERS.payment_method`, and `INGREDIENTS.unit_cost` — the minimum required to produce Tab 1 of the contractual workbook, which specifies "Event Name, Date, Duration … Net Profit Estimates … Primary Payment Distribution." The standardised terminology entry "Six-table database → Eight-table core database" is amended to **"→ Nine-table core database."**

