## 5.2 Feature Freeze & Critical Path

### 5.2.1 Feature Freeze Declaration

**Friday, 27 November 2026 is the immutable feature freeze for VenueVue Revision 2.3.**

From 27 November 2026 onward, no new functional capability, no additional report tab, no new data entity, no new endpoint, no interface redesign, and no architectural change may enter development. Work after the freeze is restricted to exactly four categories:

1. **Defect correction** against already-frozen functionality.
2. **Deployment** of the frozen build to the client host.
3. **Documentation** — the User Operational Manual and System Installation Guide.
4. **Training and User Acceptance Testing.**

Any proposal for post-freeze scope addition must be rejected by the Project Manager regardless of its merit, and deferred to the post-turnover support window or to a future project phase. This rule exists because the residual three-week window is the project's **only** buffer; consuming it with new work removes all protection from the 18 December academic deadline.

### 5.2.2 Critical Path

```
   9-table DDL ──► API Auth/RBAC ──► Recipe Deduction ──► Idempotency ──► Export Engine ──► T01–T14 ──► FREEZE
      (G2)             (G4)               (G6)               (G7)             (G8)          (G9)
    28 Aug           25 Sep             23 Oct              6 Nov           20 Nov        27 Nov
                         │                                    │
                         └──► Master Data (G5) ──► POS Grid ───┘
                                9 Oct            16 Oct
```

**Critical path narrative.** The chain runs from the database schema through the security foundation, into the recipe deduction engine, through idempotency and the offline queue, into the reporting engine, and terminates in full verification at the freeze. Two branches feed it: master data CRUD must complete before the POS grid can be built against real menu data, and the deduction engine cannot be validated until CRUD provides recipes. No task on this chain can be deferred, parallelised away, or shortened without moving the freeze date.

### 5.2.3 Zero-Float Analysis

Float is the amount of delay a task may absorb before it delays the project end date. The audit of predecessor revisions established that the schedule contained no float anywhere prior to the freeze. This condition is unchanged and is stated here explicitly so that it is managed rather than discovered.

| Critical Segment | Window | Duration | Float |
|---|---|---|---|
| G2 → G4 | 28 Aug → 25 Sep 2026 | 4 weeks | **0** |
| G4 → G6 | 25 Sep → 23 Oct 2026 | 4 weeks | **0** |
| G6 → G7 | 23 Oct → 6 Nov 2026 | 2 weeks | **0** |
| G7 → G8 | 6 Nov → 20 Nov 2026 | 2 weeks | **0** |
| G8 → G9 *(all fourteen scenarios)* | 20 Nov → 27 Nov 2026 | **1 week** | **0** |
| **G9 → G11 *(reserved)* | 27 Nov → 18 Dec 2026 | 3 weeks | **3 weeks — the only buffer** |

**Interpretation.** Every segment from 28 August to 27 November is on a zero-float chain. A single day lost anywhere in that chain moves the freeze date by one day. Because the freeze cannot move (it is bounded by the 18 December academic deadline three weeks later), any slippage in the zero-float region must be recovered through de-scoping, not through rescheduling.

**Residual risk statement.** The three-week post-freeze reserve protects *sign-off* — deployment, training, documentation, UAT, and manuscript preparation — but it **cannot absorb development slippage**, because the freeze itself sits at the end of the zero-float chain. This is a structural property of the schedule, not a temporary condition, and it is the reason the interventions below are mandatory rather than advisory.

### 5.2.4 Mandatory Schedule Interventions

The following interventions are approved and are already reflected in §5.1. They exist to recover contingency on a chain that structurally has none.

| # | Intervention | Rationale | Effect |
|---|---|---|---|
| **I-1** | **Author the T01–T14 test plan in Sprint 5 (9 October), not Sprint 9.** | Tests written alongside the engine are executable the day the engine lands. End-loaded verification is the dominant schedule risk. | Recovers approximately two weeks of contingency; converts verification from a one-week cliff into continuous practice. |
| **I-2** | **Begin executing T01–T03 immediately upon the recipe engine committing (23 October).** | The three foundational scenarios validate the core transactional claim of the system. Discovering a defect in late November would leave no recovery path. | Removes roughly two weeks of end-loaded test risk. |
| **I-3** | **Split Phase 8 across two sprints.** Tabs 1–2 by 13 November; Tabs 3–4 plus backup by 20 November. | A failure in the final two report tabs must not be able to consume the entire verification week. | Isolates reporting risk from verification capacity. |
| **I-4** | **Timebox gate G4.** If the security foundation has not passed by **2 October 2026**, invoke de-scoping. | G4 already carries five days of slippage at baseline and gates the entire critical path. | Bounds the blast radius of the highest-risk gate. |
| **I-5** | **Pre-agreed de-scoping order.** | De-scoping decisions made under time pressure and without a pre-agreed order produce arbitrary loss of value. | See §5.2.5. |

### 5.2.5 Pre-Agreed De-Scoping Order

If the zero-float chain forces de-scoping, items are cut in the following order. The sequence is deliberately arranged so that the lowest client value is sacrificed first and no cut touches the client's headline requirement.

| Order | Item De-Scoped | Justification | Objectives Preserved |
|---|---|---|---|
| **1** | PDF report export | Never in scope. Approved reporting is the four-tab `.xlsx` workbook only; the Letter of Agreement reference to PDF is struck by §6.2. No requirement depends on it. | All |
| **2** | Mechanism 3 — Manual Excel/CSV batch ingestion | Least-used of the three ingestion mechanisms. Mechanisms 1 and 2 fully satisfy the objective, and a USB JSON recovery package already provides the physical fallback. | 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 7, 8 |
| **3** | Multi-tablet concurrency testing beyond two devices | The client operates with at most three tablets. Two-device concurrency testing exercises the same row-locking path. | All |
| **4** | Cosmetic interface polish and theming | Presentation refinement carries no functional or contractual obligation. | All |

**Protected items — never de-scoped.** The following may not be cut under any schedule pressure, because each is either a contractual obligation or the client's headline requirement:

- The **nine-table schema**, in particular `EVENTS`, `ORDERS.event_id`, `ORDERS.payment_method`, and `INGREDIENTS.unit_cost`.
- **Tab 1 of the workbook with per-event net profit** — the client's Priority Rank 5 requirement and the primary client-facing value of the engagement.
- **UUIDv4 idempotency** — without it, duplicate orders and duplicate stock deductions are guaranteed.
- **API-level authentication and RBAC** — without it, the ingest endpoint is open to any device on the hotspot.
- **Atomic deduction with row locking** — without it, concurrent tablets corrupt inventory.
- **The 27 November feature freeze.**

