| New §1.4 | *(absent)* | Add: *"Host Dependency Disclosure: The Industry Client acknowledges that VenueVue operates as a locally networked system in which the host laptop functions as the single authoritative server, and that loss of the host during an event suspends system operation. The Industry Client agrees to maintain a secondary paper fallback procedure for host failure, as provided in Project Plan §2.2.3."* | The system has a documented single point of failure. Disclosing it contractually protects both parties and ensures the client maintains the operational fallback that the May 2026 Project Technical Documentation already contemplates. |
| §1.3 | *"Database Backup Utility: Build local daily database export capabilities (`.sql` files and external USB exports)…"* | **Restore the automated backup requirement to the Project Plan.** No amendment to the Agreement text is required; rather, NFR-04 has been restored to this Project Plan to match the Agreement. | The automated daily backup requirement was deleted from the plan in a prior revision while the Agreement continued to promise it. Revision 2.3 restores NFR-04 and assigns it to a named owner in Sprint 8 (task 8.6). |
| §3.1.3 | *"Provide complimentary bug fixing and system maintenance for a period of six (6) months following official system turnover."* | Append: *"Support comprises remediation of defects in delivered functionality at no charge. New feature requests fall outside the scope of this Agreement and require separate written agreement."* | Bounds the support obligation. The six-month window previously had no owner, no effort allocation, and no scope boundary, creating an open-ended commitment. Task 10.7 now assigns the window to a named owner. |

### 6.2.2 Scope Boundaries

**In Scope.** The following capabilities constitute the contractual and academic scope of VenueVue Revision 2.3:

1. User authentication with API-level role-based access control across all devices.
2. Touch-optimised POS ordering interface with category tab filtering and single-tap item selection.
3. Real-time cart handling with automatic cash change calculation.
4. Server-side authoritative price resolution.
5. Atomic recipe-based multi-ingredient depletion under row-level locking.
6. Ingredient-level inventory tracking in grams, millilitres, and discrete units.
7. Recipe setup engine mapping raw materials to menu items.
8. Automated low-stock visual alerting with configurable thresholds and a restock list.
9. UUIDv4 idempotency guaranteeing exactly-once transaction ingestion.
10. Multi-mechanism data ingestion: real-time LAN POST, IndexedDB queue with USB recovery, and manual Excel/CSV batch import.
11. **Event management and per-event profitability analytics**, including event name, venue, date, duration, gross revenue, cost of goods sold, and net profit.
12. Automated generation of four-tab Excel workbooks via PhpSpreadsheet.
13. Automated daily `.sql` database backup with USB mirroring.
14. Append-only inventory movement ledger and security audit trail.
15. **The nine-table relational database schema.**

**Out of Scope.** The following are expressly excluded and will not be delivered:

| Excluded Item | Justification |
|---|---|
| Physical receipt printing | TravelBean MNL event customers do not request printed receipts. |
| PDF report export | Not specified, scheduled, or implemented in any revision; struck from the Letter of Agreement by §6.2.1. |
| Online cloud synchronisation | Excluded by ADR-001; the system's defining purpose is independence from cloud infrastructure. |
| Any hosted database or managed authentication provider | Excluded by ADR-001 §2.1.1 and §2.2.4. |
| Hardware barcode scanning | Coffee menu items are selected directly through a touchscreen grid. |
| Customer loyalty programmes | Not applicable to a cash-only, single-event pop-up operation. |
| Integrated e-wallet payment processing | The client operates on a cash-only basis; `payment_method` is constrained to `cash`. |
| Simultaneous multi-venue cloud synchronisation | The client operates one event at a time. |
| Kitchen display and order-preparation workflow states | Not applicable to a mobile coffee stand. |
| Supplier, purchase-order, or goods-received-note management | Outside the client's stated operating model. |
| Shift management, cash-drawer reconciliation, and Z-readings | Not requested by the client. |
| Native mobile applications | Retired by ADR-001. |
| Item modifiers or size variants | Not modelled in any approved schema; FR-02's historical reference to "item options" is withdrawn. If size or add-on variants are required, a modifiers table must be added by formal scope change. |
| Tax or VAT computation | Not applicable to the client's current registration status. |

### 6.2.3 Data Privacy Act Compliance (Republic Act No. 10173)

Letter of Agreement §5.1 commits both parties to adherence to the Data Privacy Act of 2012. Revision 2.3 specifies the concrete technical controls that discharge that commitment.

| Control | Implementation | Verification |
|---|---|---|
| **Credential protection** | Passwords are stored exclusively as `password_hash()` output with `PASSWORD_DEFAULT` (bcrypt). No plaintext or reversibly-encrypted credential is ever persisted. Authentication uses `password_verify()`. | Schema inspection at G2; code review at G4. |
| **Session token protection** | Only the SHA-256 hash of each bearer token is stored in `api_sessions`. A database disclosure therefore yields no usable session credential. Tokens carry explicit expiry. | Code review at G4; `SELECT` against `api_sessions` confirms hash-only storage. |
| **Access control** | API-level RBAC enforced server-side on every request. UI-level button hiding is treated as a user-experience feature and never as an authorization control. Unauthorized requests receive HTTP 401 or HTTP 403 and are logged. | Scenarios T10 and T11 at G9. |
| **Injection prevention** | All database access uses PDO prepared statements with `PDO::ATTR_EMULATE_PREPARES => false`, guaranteeing genuine server-side preparation. | Code review; static inspection of every query at G4. |
| **Audit trail** | Every authentication outcome, authorization denial, administrative override, and committed transaction is written to the append-only `audit_logs` table with acting user, action, entity, structured detail, and client IP address. | Schema and code review; `audit_logs` population verified at G6. |
| **Data minimisation** | The system stores only operational data required for POS and inventory functions: no customer names, contact details, payment-card data, or other personal information is captured. Payment is cash-only and no payment instrument is recorded. | Schema inspection at G2; confirms absence of any personal-data column. |
| **Data localisation** | All data resides on the client's host laptop within the client's premises. No data is transmitted to any third party, and no hosted service is permitted by ADR-001. | WAN-traffic monitoring during a full sales cycle at every gate. |
| **Credential hygiene** | No credential, connection string, or token is committed to version control. The application connects using a least-privilege database account granted `SELECT`, `INSERT`, and `UPDATE` only — with no `DELETE` and no `DROP` privilege. | Repository inspection at G4; `SHOW GRANTS` at G10. |
| **Backup protection** | Automated daily `.sql` dumps are retained on the host drive and mirrored to an external USB drive kept separately from the laptop. | Backup presence verification at G8 and weekly thereafter. |
| **Breach remediation** | The `.env` file previously committed to a public repository is removed from version control and its publishable credential rotated, so the historical commit is rendered worthless. Demonstration accounts with known passwords are purged. | Repository and provider-console inspection; recorded as a gate condition at G4. |

### 6.2.4 Intellectual Property and Academic Rights

Letter of Agreement §4 is confirmed without amendment. Under the Intellectual Property Code of the Philippines (Republic Act No. 8293) and Cavite State University academic guidelines:

1. **Source code ownership.** CTRL + STAY retains full legal ownership, copyright, and intellectual property rights over the source code, system architecture, database structure, and original interface design of VenueVue.
2. **Academic presentation rights.** CTRL + STAY and Cavite State University reserve the right to demonstrate the system, publish non-confidential screenshots, present methodologies, and submit findings for academic defences, competitions, research journals, and institutional archives.
3. **Client data ownership.** All operational data, sales figures, inventory records, and transactional records generated through TravelBean MNL's use of VenueVue remain the exclusive property of TravelBean MNL.
4. **Third-party components.** All third-party libraries used — React, TanStack Router, Vite, Tailwind CSS, PHP, MySQL, XAMPP, and PhpSpreadsheet — are used under their respective open-source licences. No proprietary or licence-encumbered component is introduced.

### 6.2.5 Governing Law

This Project Plan and the Letter of Agreement it supports are governed by, construed, and enforced in accordance with the laws of the Republic of the Philippines, including the Civil Code of the Philippines (R.A. 386), the Intellectual Property Code (R.A. 8293), and the Data Privacy Act of 2012 (R.A. 10173).

